<h2>The dispute</h2>
<p>The petitioner, a 73-year-old widow, said that she and her late husband had adopted the first respondent, raised him and arranged his marriage. Her husband left a will giving the property to the son while protecting her right to live in the house for her lifetime. After a dispute and an alleged assault on 30 December 2024, she said she was pushed out of the house and forced to stay with a relative.</p>
<p>The Judicial Magistrate (Mahila Court), Puducherry, in D.V.C. No. 8 of 2026, found a prima facie domestic relationship and a shared household, and granted a residence order and a protection order under Sections 17 and 19 of the DV Act. On 13 July 2026, however, the III Additional Sessions Judge stayed that order on the condition that the son pay Rs. 15,000 per month towards alternative accommodation. The widow carried the matter to the High Court in criminal revision.</p>

<h2>What the High Court held</h2>
<p>Justice Shamim Ahmed stayed the appellate order and restored the Magistrate&rsquo;s residence and protection orders with immediate effect. Three strands of reasoning stand out.</p>

<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><h3>Immediate protection</h3><p>DV proceedings exist to provide urgent, practical protection. The machinery of the Act cannot be made to wait for the outcome of slower civil litigation about the same property.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Title is for the civil court</h3><p>Questions of ownership, the validity of the will, sale deed and settlement deed were expressly left open. A DV court protects possession and residence; it does not decide title.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Money is not residence</h3><p>A monthly payment may not adequately protect an elderly woman&rsquo;s right to reside in her own shared household. The payment was continued, but in addition to — not instead of — restoration of residence.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The protective directions</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Restoration</strong> — the residence and protection orders of the Mahila Court stand restored and are to be implemented forthwith.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Official assistance</strong> — the District Magistrate and the Senior Superintendent of Police, Puducherry, are to help the petitioner return safely to the residence.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Continuing payment</strong> — the son must keep paying Rs. 15,000 per month, due by the 15th of each month from October 2026.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Conduct</strong> — the respondents must not obstruct her residence, dispossess her or subject her to abuse, and must provide food, clothing and medical care.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Monitoring</strong> — a police constable and a woman constable are to visit daily, with compliance reports to the court; the matter is listed again on 29 October 2026.</div>
</div>

<h2>Why the decision matters</h2>
<p>Residence orders are among the most contested reliefs under the DV Act, precisely because they bite immediately. Respondents frequently offer — and appellate courts sometimes accept — a monetary arrangement in place of actual restoration. This order is a reminder that the two reliefs are distinct. Section 19 protects the aggrieved person&rsquo;s occupation of the shared household; Section 20 addresses her financial needs. Where the aggrieved person is elderly, has a will-protected life interest, and alleges forcible dispossession, the balance tilts strongly toward restoring residence rather than monetising it.</p>
<div class="note">
<p>The order also illustrates the growing convergence between the DV Act and the Maintenance and Welfare of Parents and Senior Citizens Act, 2007. Courts increasingly read the two regimes together where the aggrieved woman is an aged parent, taking a protective approach while leaving property rights to be worked out in civil proceedings.</p>
</div>
<p>For practitioners on the respondent&rsquo;s side, the lesson is equally clear: a stay of a residence order obtained in appeal is vulnerable if it rests on a payment alone. Any arrangement offered in substitution must engage with the aggrieved person&rsquo;s actual housing security, her age and health, and the source of her right to reside — not merely her monthly outgoings.</p>
